/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Three sources add up.
   - ΔT is the adiabatic liquid temperature rise in K; Gs the solute absorbed in kmol/h; ΔHs the heat released per kmol solute in kJ/kmol; L the liquid flow in kmol/h; cp the liquid heat capacity in kJ/(kmol·K).
   - Mass form: ΔT = (Gs ΔHs)/(ṁ cp), with ṁ in kg/h and cp in kJ/(kg·K).
   - Henry's constant H rises with temperature, so m = H/P rises.
   - On Y–X coordinates the operating line uses solute-free flows Ls and Gs, which stay constant, so the line is straight even for a concentrated tower.
   - Two routes work.
   - Practical fixes are cheaper than a taller tower:
   - Stripping is the mirror image.
   - Answer.
*/
