/* book Worked example 2: Q: A lean-oil absorber with N = 4 theoretical stages treats 100 kmol/h of gas containing 8 kmol/h ethane, 5 kmol/h propane, 3 kmol/h n-butane and 1 kmol/h n-pentane (the rest methane). Fresh oil enters at L/G = 1.0 (dilute approximation). The equilibrium slopes at column conditions are m = 3.6 (ethane), 1.25 (propane), 0.45 (n-butane) and 0.16 (n-pentane). Find the fraction and amount of each solute absorbed.
   1. Ai = (L/G)/mi: ethane 0.278, propane 0.800, n-butane 2.222, n-pentane 6.25.
   2. Fraction absorbed φ = (A5 − A)/(A5 − 1), since N + 1 = 5.
   3. Ethane: A⁵ = 0.00165; φ = (0.00165 − 0.278)/(0.00165 − 1) = 0.277, so 8 × 0.277 = 2.21 kmol/h absorbed. Almost exactly A: below 1, the limit is A.
   4. Propane: A⁵ = 0.328; φ = (0.328 − 0.800)/(0.328 − 1) = 0.702, so 3.51 kmol/h.
   5. n-Butane: A⁵ = 54.2; φ = (54.2 − 2.22)/(54.2 − 1) = 0.977, so 2.93 kmol/h.
   6. n-Pentane: φ = 0.999, so 1.00 kmol/h.
   Answer: Absorbed: ethane 27.7 % (2.21 kmol/h), propane 70.2 % (3.51), n-butane 97.7 % (2.93), n-pentane 99.9 % (1.00). One L/G and one N serve all four; only mi differs. */
