/* book exam traps: Using one average Henry constant for a mixture. Each solute has its own mi, so each has its own equilibrium line. | Setting a separate L/G for each solute. One liquid and one gas flow serve every component; the key component fixes the shared L/G. | Applying E = Ha outside the fast-reaction range. E ≈ Ha is valid only when Ha is above about 3 and much smaller than Ei; for slow reaction E is near 1, and when Ha is comparable to or larger than Ei use E = Ei. | Mixing units in Ha: k must be in s⁻¹ for pseudo-first-order reaction, DA in m²/s, and kL in m/s. | Using Ei for a slow reaction; it applies only when the reaction is far faster than diffusion and B is in the liquid. */
